{"_data": [{"contents": [{"content": "Sports and Leisure", "type": "lesson_name"}, {"content": "America's Pastime", "type": "unit_name"}, {"content": 2, "type": "position"}, {"content": "stub", "type": "origin"}, {"content": 2, "type": "lesson"}, {"content": "B2.2", "type": "level"}], "type": "metadata"}, {"contents": [{"content": "false", "type": "test"}], "type": "achievment_test"}, {"contents": [{"content": "Describe detailed facts about events or actions in work and social contexts, in written or spoken situations.", "type": "primary"}, {"content": "Describing the Effects of Something. ", "type": "secondary_1"}, {"content": "Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_2"}, {"content": "Describing the Effects of Something.", "type": "secondary_3"}, {"content": " Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_4"}, {"content": "Describing the Effects of Something.", "type": "secondary_5"}, {"content": " Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_6"}, {"content": "Describing the Effects of Something.", "type": "secondary_7"}, {"content": " Understands general and detailed information, including specialized articles of the professional or academic life on a wide range of general and work topics, in conversations or presentations, spoken at a natural speed and in circumstances that make it more difficult to hear.", "type": "secondary_8"}], "type": "objectives"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02re1img01.jpg", "type": "background_image"}, {"contents": [{"content": " Describing the Effects of Something", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use adjectives to describe the effects of something on a person.<br/> Adjectives ending in <span class=\"strong\">-ing</span> show the effect that something has on a person.<br/> Adjectives ending in <span class=\"strong\">-ed</span> show how the person has been affected by the situation or thing. ", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Adjectives ending in <span class=\"strong\">-ed</span> can only be used to describe feelings or emotions. They describe how a person feels. They cannot be used to describe an object or situation.<br/> Adjectives ending in <span class=\"strong\">-ing</span> can be used either with a person or a situation. They describe the characteristics of a person or a situation. They also describe how a situation makes you feel. ", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Compare the differences:<br/> <ul><li>John is bored. (John feels bored.)<br/> <li>John is boring. (John is a boring person.)<br/> <li>The movie is boring. (The movie causes people to feel bored.)<br/></li></ul> You cannot say <span class=\"italic\">the movie is bored</span>, since the subject is not a person.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>I was <span class=\"strong\">excited</span> by how <span class=\"strong\">exciting</span> the baseball game was yesterday.<br/> <li>John was <span class=\"strong\">bored</span> by the <span class=\"strong\">boring</span> race he saw last night.<br/> <li>That football game was <span class=\"strong\">tiring</span>. I felt really <span class=\"strong\">tired</span> last night.</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Today baseball is one of the most captivating symbols of North American culture.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The viewing public was captivated by players such as Babe Ruth.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": " Simple Past Passive to Report Events", "type": "heading"}, {"content": "You use the active voice when you want the \u2018doer' of the action to be the subject.<br/> You use the passive voice when you want the \u2018receiver' of the action to be the subject.<br/><br/>  You can use the passive voice when you want to report events that happened in the past in a general way.", "type": "describer"}, {"content": "Most of spoken English uses active speech. When you use an active sentence to report an event, the subject is the focus of the sentence.<br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>Newspapers reported that 40 million Americans (subject) <span class=\"strong\">watched</span> the final game in 1986 (object). </li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "A passive sentence puts the emphasis on the object of the active sentence. When you use a passive sentence to report an event, the object of the active sentence now becomes the subject and focus of the passive sentence.<br/> Example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>The final game in 1986 (subject)  <span class=\"strong\">was reported to have been watched by</span> 40 million Americans (object).</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You make the passive form of a verb by using tenses of the <span class=\"italic\">be</span> verb followed by the past participle.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "You use the structure:<br/>subject + Simple Past passive reporting verb + To-Infinitive (Perfect or Perfect Continuous tense)", "type": "text"}, {"content": "For example:<br/> <span class=\"italic\"><ul><li>A baseball-like game <span class=\"strong\">was said to have been played by</span> North American children in the 1800s.<br/> <li>Babe Ruth <span class=\"strong\">was said to have been able to hit</span> a ball over 600 feet.</li></ul></span>", "type": "text"}, {"content": "The first game was claimed to have been recorded by Abner Doubleday in Cooperstown, New York.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "The rules were said to have been standardized by Alexander Cartwright in 1845.", "type": "example"}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"content": "to view", "type": "name"}, {"content": "to watch", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(verb)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Professional baseball games are viewed live and on TV by millions of people of different backgrounds and ages.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud01", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": " These are words you use to talk about baseball.", "type": "text"}], "type": "target_word_describer"}, {"contents": [{"content": "viewership ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "the total number of people who watch something, usually on TV", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Viewership for the final game of the 1986 championship was reported to have exceeded forty million.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud02", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "slugger ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "baseball player who hits the ball with power", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In the United States some baseball sluggers are more recognizable than politicians. ", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud03", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "pastime", "type": "name"}, {"content": "hobby; activity done for enjoyment", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: Baseball is not just America's pastime anymore, either.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud04", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "base ", "type": "name"}, {"content": "a small, square cushion that a player must touch in order to score run", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: In the beginning the rules were more of a confused collection of guidelines, with surprising inconsistencies in the number of bases, outs, and innings.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud05", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "out", "type": "name"}, {"content": "player who has been retired by the opposing team", "type": "definition"}, {"content": "(noun)", "type": "part_speech"}, {"content": "Example: The team needed just three outs to end the game.", "type": "example"}, {"content": "engenn10le02ob02aud06", "type": "audio"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"content": "On a blank piece of paper, draw a picture of a baseball diamond.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Go online to find the names of each position and each location (e.g. pitcher's mound, left field).", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Label each position on your diagram and write a short definition.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "Show your picture dictionary to a partner and explain each word.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_extension"}, {"contents": [{"content": "I research baseball vocabulary.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I define the words used to talk about baseball.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I present information in a visual dictionary.", "type": "text"}, {"content": "I share information with a partner.", "type": "text"}], "type": "takeaway_assessment"}], "labs": [{"stimuli": [{"template_override": 1, "contents": [{}], "_data": [], "id": 0, "type": "welcome"}, {"template_override": 1, "_data": [], "title": "", "type": "audio", "contents": [{"value": {"images": [{"image": "engenn10le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "time": "0", "id": 0, "text": ""}, {"image": "engenn10le02ob02re2img01.jpg", "time": "100", "id": 1, "text": "Today, baseball is one of the most captivating symbols in North American culture. Professional baseball games are viewed live and on TV by millions of people of different backgrounds and ages. Viewership for the final game of the 1986 championship was reported to have exceeded forty million. There are \u201clittle leagues\u201d for children and \u201cbeer leagues\u201d for amateur adults in almost every town. Foreigners are always surprised to find that in the United States some baseball sluggers are more recognizable than politicians."}, {"image": "engenn10le02ob02re2img02.jpg", "time": "34000", "id": 2, "text": "A baseball-like game was said to have been played by North American children in the early 1800s, and the first game was claimed to have been recorded by Abner Doubleday in Cooperstown, New York. This myth has also been debunked, and it was eventually discovered that the first game was actually played in Beachville. In the beginning the rules were more of a confused collection of guidelines, with surprising inconsistencies in the number of bases, outs, and innings. The rules were said to have been standardized by Alexander Cartwright in 1845, and soon after, a number of baseball clubs were formed. Games were played in cities around the country and the baseball fan was born. People began to follow their favorite players and teams. The viewing public was captivated by players such as Babe Ruth, who was said to have been able to hit a ball over 600 feet."}, {"image": "engenn10le02ob02re2img03.jpg", "time": "90000", "id": 3, "text": "It is not difficult to understand the popularity of baseball. There is a small amount of equipment needed and the rules are not confusing. It is a sport that can be played by almost everyone. It's not just America's pastime anymore, either. Baseball is enjoyed in dozens of countries, from Japan to the Dominican Republic."}], "audiosrc": "engenn10le02ob02re2aud01", "coverimg": "engenn10le02ob02re2img01.jpg"}, "type": "slideshow"